==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰreg-}}
From {{der|en|la|refrāctum}}, neuter form of {{m|la|refrāctus}}, the past participle of {{m|la|refringō}}, itself from {{m|la|re-|t=again}} + {{m|la|frangō|t=I break}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-refract.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ækt|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|physics}} Of a [[medium]], substance, object, etc.: to [[deflect]] the course of ([[lightrays|light rays]]), esp. when they enter the medium, etc., at an [[oblique]] angle; to cause refraction of (light, other [[electromagnetic radiation]], or sound or other [[wave]] phenomena).
# {{lb|en|transitive|figurative}} To [[mediate]]; to [[alter]]; to [[distort]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|optics}} To cause (light) to change direction as a result of entering a different medium.
# {{lb|en|transitive|ophthalmology}} To measure, and often also to correct with lenses, the refractive error of (an eye) or the eyes of (a person).
#: {{ux|en|A prism can '''refract''' light.}}

